WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Welcome. In this class, you will turn an
adaptable goal

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
into a bounded agent run. You will decide
whether an

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
agent is justified, write a work order
that removes hidden

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
decisions, separate trusted authority from
untrusted observations, make every

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
transition visible, and stop with truthful
evidence when the system

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
completes, needs help, runs out of budget,
or cannot determine

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
whether an action succeeded.

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.833
Begin by asking whether the path is known.
A deterministic

9
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:46.667
workflow uses code-defined steps and
branches. A model may help

10
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:52.500
classify or draft inside one step, but
code still decides

11
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:58.333
what happens next. A model-directed loop
is useful when observations

12
00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:04.167
change the next reasonable action and
enumerating every path is

13
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:10.000
impractical. That flexibility creates more
states, failure modes, and evidence

14
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.833
requirements. Start with the simplest
design that can meet the

15
00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:21.667
outcome. Add an agent only when
representative cases show that

16
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:27.500
fixed orchestration cannot handle
necessary variation. The word agent is

17
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:33.333
not a quality measure. A reliable fixed
workflow is better

18
00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:39.167
than an adaptable loop that cannot prove
what it changed

19
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:45.000
or why it stopped.

20
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:51.667
Before any tool is available, write the
work order. State

21
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:58.333
the outcome as something that can become
true in the

22
00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:05.000
world, not as a vague instruction to
improve or investigate.

23
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.667
Name allowed files, systems, records, and
time range. Identify trusted

24
00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:18.333
inputs and systems of record. List
constraints and what must

25
00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:25.000
remain unchanged. Define the audience when
it changes tone, risk,

26
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.667
or verification. Then define evidence:
tests, rendered output, citations, an

27
00:02:31.667 --> 00:02:38.333
approved diff, a postcondition query, or
another observation independent of

28
00:02:38.333 --> 00:02:45.000
the model's final message. Include
non-goals so a useful idea

29
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:51.667
does not silently expand scope. Record who
may authorize a

30
00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:58.333
change in permission, cost, external
impact, or target. The work

31
00:02:58.333 --> 00:03:05.000
order is the run's authority boundary and
success contract.

32
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.455
Consider the request, update the support
guide. It hides several

33
00:03:10.455 --> 00:03:15.909
decisions. A bounded version says: update
only the password-reset guide

34
00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:21.364
for the current documented process; use
the approved policy and

35
00:03:21.364 --> 00:03:26.818
tested application behavior as sources; do
not change policy, account

36
00:03:26.818 --> 00:03:32.273
permissions, or other guides; preserve
accessibility and links; finish only

37
00:03:32.273 --> 00:03:37.727
when the content diff is reviewed, links
pass, and the

38
00:03:37.727 --> 00:03:43.182
rendered page matches the approved
process. If the sources conflict,

39
00:03:43.182 --> 00:03:48.636
stop for the policy owner. If a new
authentication exception

40
00:03:48.636 --> 00:03:54.091
is needed, stop because that changes
policy. The agent can

41
00:03:54.091 --> 00:03:59.545
now adapt its research and editing steps
without receiving permission

42
00:03:59.545 --> 00:04:05.000
to rewrite the entire support system.

43
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:11.818
Keep authority separate from observations.
A web page, issue, document,

44
00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:18.636
log, retrieved passage, tool result,
memory entry, or message from

45
00:04:18.636 --> 00:04:25.455
another agent may contain instructions.
Those instructions are data to

46
00:04:25.455 --> 00:04:32.273
analyze unless a trusted authority
deliberately placed them in the

47
00:04:32.273 --> 00:04:39.091
work order or governing policy. Label
provenance, trust, freshness, and

48
00:04:39.091 --> 00:04:45.909
sensitivity. If retrieved text says to
ignore an approval, change

49
00:04:45.909 --> 00:04:52.727
the destination, reveal a secret, or
broaden access, reject that

50
00:04:52.727 --> 00:04:59.545
instruction and record the conflict.
Low-risk ambiguity may permit a

51
00:04:59.545 --> 00:05:06.364
documented assumption when the work order
allows it. Ambiguity that

52
00:05:06.364 --> 00:05:13.182
changes scope, permission, cost, target,
or external impact requires authorization.

53
00:05:13.182 --> 00:05:20.000
More context cannot grant more authority.

54
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.818
Represent the run as state, not as a
conversation. Record

55
00:05:26.818 --> 00:05:33.636
the contract version, current objective,
verified facts, unresolved evidence,

56
00:05:33.636 --> 00:05:40.455
completed action identifiers, approvals,
remaining budgets, and next allowed

57
00:05:40.455 --> 00:05:47.273
actions. Each iteration inspects the
environment, proposes a decision,

58
00:05:47.273 --> 00:05:54.091
validates identity and authority, executes
at most one bounded action,

59
00:05:54.091 --> 00:06:00.909
verifies the postcondition, and records a
transition. The controller accepts

60
00:06:00.909 --> 00:06:07.727
finish only when independent evidence
satisfies the success contract. A

61
00:06:07.727 --> 00:06:14.545
fluent summary is not a terminal signal.
For consequential actions,

62
00:06:14.545 --> 00:06:21.364
the transition must distinguish requested,
authorized, attempted, accepted,

63
00:06:21.364 --> 00:06:28.182
completed, failed, and outcome unknown.
That distinction prevents a timeout

64
00:06:28.182 --> 00:06:35.000
from being treated as proof that nothing
happened.

65
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:41.818
Define terminal outcomes before execution.
Succeeded means every required

66
00:06:41.818 --> 00:06:48.636
postcondition passed. Failed means the run
stopped and the evidence

67
00:06:48.636 --> 00:06:55.455
confirms the requested outcome was not
achieved. Unknown means a

68
00:06:55.455 --> 00:07:02.273
consequential effect cannot yet be
reconciled. Needs approval means the

69
00:07:02.273 --> 00:07:09.091
next exact action exceeds current
authority. Refused means the request

70
00:07:09.091 --> 00:07:15.909
violates policy. Cancelled means an
authorized cancellation was observed.

71
00:07:15.909 --> 00:07:22.727
Budget exceeded means a hard limit ended
the run. Repeated

72
00:07:22.727 --> 00:07:29.545
non-progress means equivalent actions
changed neither state nor evidence. These

73
00:07:29.545 --> 00:07:36.364
outcomes must not collapse into success or
a generic error.

74
00:07:36.364 --> 00:07:43.182
Each one tells the learner or operator
what is known,

75
00:07:43.182 --> 00:07:50.000
what remains uncertain, and which recovery
actions are permitted.

76
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:57.000
Choose one agent task. Write its
independently observable success

77
00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:04.000
condition, one exact approval boundary,
one no-progress rule, one budget

78
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:11.000
limit, and the evidence required to report
an unknown outcome.

79
00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:46.600
Checkpoint. A research agent has tried
three nearly identical searches.

80
00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:52.200
Each returned the same two documents,
neither contains the required

81
00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:57.800
fact, and the state ledger shows no new
evidence. Six

82
00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:03.400
of ten turns remain. Should the controller
allow another equivalent

83
00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:09.000
search?

84
00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:13.500
Stop the equivalent search. The budget is
a ceiling, not

85
00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:18.000
a reason to continue. Preserve the
queries, results, evidence digest,

86
00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.500
and missing fact. Report repeated
non-progress. A materially different

87
00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:27.000
source or method may be attempted only if
it is

88
00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.500
allowed by the work order and has a
reasoned chance

89
00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:36.000
to change the evidence state. Otherwise
hand off. If you

90
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:40.500
chose to continue because turns remain,
revise your rule so

91
00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:45.000
progress depends on a meaningful state or
evidence change. If

92
00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:49.500
you stopped but discarded the search
history, preserve it so

93
00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:54.000
recovery does not repeat the same path.

94
00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:00.818
A recoverable stop preserves the contract
version, safe state references,

95
00:12:00.818 --> 00:12:07.636
verified facts, completed action
identifiers, approvals, tool results,

96
00:12:07.636 --> 00:12:14.455
postcondition evidence, open
uncertainties, budget use, and next

97
00:12:14.455 --> 00:12:21.273
permitted decisions. Reconcile the real
environment before retrying a

98
00:12:21.273 --> 00:12:28.091
consequential action. Classify prior steps
as verified complete, confirmed failed,

99
00:12:28.091 --> 00:12:34.909
unknown, or unattempted. Resume only if
current policy, credentials, data,

100
00:12:34.909 --> 00:12:41.727
tools, and targets still match the
checkpoint. A retry, rollback,

101
00:12:41.727 --> 00:12:48.545
or compensation is a new action with its
own authority

102
00:12:48.545 --> 00:12:55.364
and failure modes. A useful human handoff
states the goal,

103
00:12:55.364 --> 00:13:02.182
what was verified, what remains uncertain,
what did not occur,

104
00:13:02.182 --> 00:13:09.000
and the exact decision required.

105
00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:15.333
Open the bounded agent loop activity.
Write the success contract,

106
00:13:15.333 --> 00:13:21.667
allowed actions, trusted observations,
approval boundaries, explicit state,

107
00:13:21.667 --> 00:13:28.000
budgets, and terminal outcomes. Simulate
normal completion, three identical

108
00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:34.333
no-progress transitions, budget
exhaustion, and an untrusted observation

109
00:13:34.333 --> 00:13:40.667
containing an instruction. Retain the
transition log and exact evidence

110
00:13:40.667 --> 00:13:47.000
for every terminal result.

111
00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:51.000
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

112
00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:55.000
identify a bounded work order, classify
retrieved instructions, verify a

113
00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:59.000
finish decision, stop repeated
non-progress, and choose useful loop

114
00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:03.000
budgets. Review the class or return to the
activity before

115
00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:07.000
submitting. The assessment begins only
when you choose Begin knowledge

116
00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:11.000
check.

117
00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:15.667
A bounded agent earns flexibility through
explicit authority, observable state,

118
00:17:15.667 --> 00:17:20.333
independent evidence, hard budgets,
truthful terminal outcomes, and

119
00:17:20.333 --> 00:17:25.000
recoverable stops.
